  "generalizedRange": "Mountain rivers of central and southern China",
  "hook": "It outlasted the dinosaurs. It may not outlast the menu.",
  "story": [
    {
      "id": "salamander-size",
      "text": "The Chinese giant salamander is the largest amphibian on Earth — as long as a grown man is tall, hidden under boulders in cold mountain rivers.",
      "sourceIDs": [
        "zsl-cgs-conservation",
        "london-zoo-cgs",
        "sdz-cgs"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "salamander-lineage",
      "text": "Its lineage split from all other amphibians more than 170 million years ago, in the Jurassic.",
      "sourceIDs": [
        "zsl-worlds-largest-amphibian"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "salamander-skin",
      "text": "The adult has no gills. It breathes through its skin, and its wrinkled, baggy folds exist to add oxygen-catching surface.",
      "sourceIDs": [
        "sdz-cgs",
        "london-zoo-cgs"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "salamander-senses",
      "text": "Nearly blind, it reads the water's vibrations through sensory nodes along its body, then takes prey in one gape-and-suck strike.",
      "sourceIDs": [
        "sdz-cgs",
        "london-zoo-cgs"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "salamander-babyfish",
      "text": "Locals call it the baby fish, for the crying sound it is said to make.",
      "sourceIDs": [
        "sdz-cgs",
        "edge-hunting-baby-fish"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "salamander-survey",
      "text": "The largest wildlife survey in Chinese conservation history — 97 sites, sixteen provinces, four years — found just 24, many or all likely farm escapes.",
      "sourceIDs": [
        "zsl-cgs-conservation"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "salamander-paradox",
      "text": "That is the paradox: farms held some two hundred thousand salamanders for the luxury food market while the rivers emptied — many stocked with illegally wild-caught animals.",
      "sourceIDs": [
        "zsl-dying-for-a-delicacy",
        "zsl-worlds-largest-amphibian"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "salamander-releases",
      "text": "Well-meant releases of farmed animals carry their own dangers, spreading farm disease and blurring what genetics revealed to be seven or more distinct species.",
      "sourceIDs": [
        "zsl-cgs-conservation",
        "zsl-worlds-largest-amphibian"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "salamander-plan",
      "text": "A 2024 action plan, built with Chinese partners, now maps the route back.",
      "sourceIDs": [
        "zsl-cgs-conservation"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "salamander-witness",
      "text": "To witness the giant salamander is to stand by the oldest amphibian lineage on Earth while it can still be found.",
      "sourceIDs": [
        "zsl-worlds-largest-amphibian"
      ]
    }
  ],
